\section{Adaptation Motivation and Regularization Scaling}
\label{sec:adaptation-motivation}
\label{app:rs-scaling}

The estimator mathematics above is independent of how its bounded parameters
are scheduled.  This section asks a different question: how should a practical
marine implementation move the OU time scale, stationary acceleration scale,
and integral-state regularization as the wave regime changes?  The analysis is
used to identify sensible dimensions, time-scale similarity, and qualitative
trade-offs.  It is not presented as a closed-form optimum for the complete
21-state MEKF; the final schedule and its coefficients are selected and tested
empirically in Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}.

The starting point is the third-integral coordinate itself.  For a scalar
acceleration process with characteristic standard deviation $\sigma_{aw}$ and
time scale $\tau$, dimensional similarity gives the natural state scales
\[
a_w\sim\sigma_{aw},\qquad
v\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau,\qquad
p\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau^2,\qquad
S\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau^3.
\]
The result is made exact for the OU prior below.  Because an unregularized third
integral is not stationary, the theorem is a similarity statement; it does not
by itself determine the Kalman pseudo-measurement covariance or its optimal
closed-loop strength.

